- yash-random-y-tst-hangs: `tests/yash/random-y.tst` hangs (bounded by
  CTest's 120s per-file TIMEOUT). The rest of the suite is fine.
  `$RANDOM` itself varies correctly when checked directly, so the
  two busy-`until`-loops at the top aren't the obvious cause; more
  likely the later cases piping a function's output through subshells
  (`print > seeded1; print > seeded2; diff seeded1 seeded2`), similar
  in shape to `subshell-exit-trap-output-misdirected`. Not isolated to
  a specific line yet.

  2026-07-29: confirmed still open and unrelated to any in-tree
  change -- reproduces on a from-scratch checkout of this exact
  commit, with no local modifications. Earlier testing this same day
  claimed this (and a since-fixed, unrelated `redir-p.tst` bug,
  `fixes/89`) were sensitive to `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` vs the
  project's default `MinSizeRel` -- that turned out, for the
  `redir-p.tst` case, to be two stacked *testing* mistakes (a stale
  `enabled-builtins` comparison and a relative testee path silently
  breaking after `run-tst.sh`'s `cd`), not a real build-type
  sensitivity, so treat the same claim here with equal suspicion until
  it's re-confirmed with paths/builtins actually held constant across
  the comparison. What is solid: the hung process sits at 100% CPU (a
  spin, not a blocked syscall), which points at the two busy-`until`
  `$RANDOM`-comparison loops at the top of the file, despite the
  earlier note ruling them out from a shallower check -- next step is
  a debug build (`cfg -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug`) under a debugger to
  catch exactly which loop is spinning and why the two `$RANDOM` reads
  it compares aren't diverging.

- ln-trailing-slash-on-plain-destination: `builtin_ln()`
  (`src/builtin/builtin_ln.c`) unconditionally appends a `/` to the
  destination operand while building the link path, before ever
  checking whether that destination is a directory (`is_dir`) -- so
  `ln -s target name` (destination `name` not an existing directory)
  always ends up calling `symlink(target, "name/")`, which fails with
  `ENOTDIR`/`ENOENT` since a trailing-slash path requires the
  component before it to already be a directory. Only
  `ln -s target existing-dir/` (or a bare existing directory as
  destination) happens to work, because that path's `is_dir` branch
  overwrites `path` with `dst/basename(target)` before the trailing
  slash matters. Found while writing `tests/builtin-rm.sh` (worked
  around there via `/bin/ln`, not fixed here -- unrelated to that
  change).

    ln -s /etc/hostname mylink
    # symlink: mylink/: No such file or directory -- should silently
    # create ./mylink -> /etc/hostname

- eval-lineno-imprecise-inside-function: `$LINENO` read from within an
  `eval`'d string now counts from the invoking statement's line
  (fixed, see fixes/92) rather than always reporting 1, but the value
  it inherits (`parse_lineno`, stamped once per top-level list by
  `sh_loop()`) is the *call site's* line, not the eval statement's own
  line when eval runs from inside a function or loop body whose
  definition spans multiple lines -- there is no per-command location
  tracking to fall back on for that case (unlike a direct, non-eval'd
  `$LINENO` reference, which reads the parse-time `loc` stamped
  individually on its own `N_ARGPARAM` node). Confirmed:

    f() {
      echo $LINENO   # correctly prints 2
      eval 'echo $LINENO'   # prints 5 (f's call site), should print 3
    }
    f

- set-missing-posix-options: `builtin_set()` (`src/builtin/builtin_set.c`)
  only recognizes `-efhmuxBCH` (`-B`/`-H` are bash extensions; the rest
  are POSIX). Missing, all silently rejected as "invalid option":

    -a  allexport   (export every variable assignment, not just those
                     explicitly `export`ed)
    -b  notify      (report background job completion immediately,
                     not just before the next prompt)
    -n  noexec      (read commands but don't execute them; used by
                     e.g. `sh -n script` syntax checks)
    -v  verbose     (echo each input line as it's read, before any
                     expansion -- distinct from -x, which echoes after
                     expansion)
    -o option-name  (set/query options by long name instead of a
                     letter -- POSIX requires at least the long names
                     matching the letter options above; bash also
                     exposes options with no letter equivalent at all,
                     e.g. `pipefail`. There's also no bare `set -o` /
                     `set +o` to print current option states.)

  Also POSIX requires `-p` (privileged: don't process `$ENV`, etc. --
  relevant here since `V_EXPORT`/environment import already exist);
  not implemented either. `struct shopt` (`src/sh.h`) has no bit for
  any of these, so implementing them is more than wiring up
  `shell_getopt_r`'s optstring.

  Separately, but found while checking this: `set -` (bare `-`,
  intended to end option processing the same as `--` while also
  turning off `-x`/`-v`) leaves the `-` itself as the new `$1` instead
  of being consumed, because `shell_getopt_r()` (`lib/shell/shell_getopt.c`)
  returns -1 for a bare "-" argument without advancing `optind` past
  it:

    set - a b c; echo "$1 $2 $3"
    # prints "- a b", should print "a b c"

- set-errexit-not-enforced: `struct shopt`'s `errexit` bit (`src/sh.h`,
  `-e`) is set correctly by `set -e`/`set +e` and reflected back
  correctly in `$-` (`src/sh/sh_fmtflags.c`), but nothing else in the
  codebase ever reads it -- not `src/eval/`, not `src/sh/sh_loop.c`.
  A failing command never aborts the script, no matter how `set -e`
  is documented in `help_set` (`src/builtin/builtin_set.c`):

    set -e; false; echo "still ran"
    # prints "still ran" -- should exit before reaching echo

- set-noglob-not-enforced: `struct shopt`'s `noglob` bit (`src/sh.h`,
  `-f`) is set correctly by `set -f`/`set +f` and reflected back
  correctly in `$-`, but `src/expand/expand_glob.c` (and everything
  else) never checks it -- pathname expansion always runs regardless:

    set -f; echo *.c
    # still expands *.c -- should print the literal "*.c"

- set-hashall-not-enforced: `struct shopt`'s `hashall` bit (`src/sh.h`,
  `-h`) is set correctly by `set -h`/`set +h` and reflected back
  correctly in `$-`, but nothing in `src/exec/` ever checks it --
  command locations are always remembered on first lookup regardless
  of this flag. The default is even "on" (`src/sh/sh_root.c`), so
  turning it "off" can't be observed at all.

- set-braceexpand-not-enforced: `struct shopt`'s `braceexpand` bit
  (`src/sh.h`, `-B`) is set correctly by `set -B`/`set +B` and
  reflected back correctly in `$-`, but neither `src/parse/parse_param.c`
  nor `src/parse/parse_unquoted.c` (where brace expansion is actually
  parsed) ever checks it -- behavior is identical with the flag on or
  off.

- set-histexpand-unimplemented: `struct shopt`'s `histexpand` bit
  (`src/sh.h`, `-H`) is set correctly by `set -H`/`set +H` and
  reflected back correctly in `$-`, but nothing checks it either --
  more fundamentally, there is no history-expansion ('!') feature
  implemented at all under `src/history/` or `src/term/` for this
  flag to gate. It's a placeholder with nothing behind it.

- squoted-backslash-newline-swallowed: `source_skip()`/`source_peekn()`
  (`src/source/source_skip.c`, `src/source/source_peekn.c`) treat a
  backslash immediately followed by a newline as a line continuation
  and silently remove both bytes, unconditionally, before any
  quoting-mode parser ever sees the raw bytes. That's correct outside
  quotes and inside double quotes (see fixes/93), but POSIX requires
  single quotes to preserve *every* character literally, with no
  exceptions -- a backslash-newline inside `'...'` must stay exactly
  as written, not vanish. `parse_squoted.c` reads through `source_get()`,
  which goes through the same swallowing primitives, so it inherits
  this bug too:

    printf "%s\n" "X='a\\" 'b'"'" > /tmp/t.sh   # X='a\<newline>b'
    . /tmp/t.sh; printf '%s' "$X" | od -c
    # prints "a b" (2 bytes) -- should print "a\<newline>b" (4 bytes:
    # 'a', '\', newline, 'b')

  Fixing this properly means teaching `source_peek()`/`source_skip()`
  (or their callers) to skip the continuation-removal step while
  `p->quot == Q_SQUOTED` -- these primitives are shared by every
  quoting mode and by unquoted parsing, so the fix has to be more
  careful than the narrowly-scoped one in fixes/93.

- unpaired-bracket-triggers-real-glob-every-time: `parse_unquoted.c`'s
  glob-flag heuristic sets `S_GLOB` on a word the instant it sees
  *any* unquoted character in `parse_isesc()`'s class (`* ? [ ] \`),
  with no check for whether those characters actually form a
  syntactically plausible pattern:

      /* if it is a character subject to globbing then set S_GLOB flag */
      else if(parse_isesc(c)) {
        flags |= S_GLOB;
      }

  A lone `[` with no matching `]` in the same word can never validly
  match anything as a bracket expression, but `S_GLOB` gets set for it
  anyway, so `expand_glob()` (`src/expand/expand_glob.c`) calls the
  real libc `glob(3)` on it at expansion time regardless -- which,
  because glob() can't take the "no metacharacters, just stat() it"
  fast path once it *sees* a `[`, falls through to actually opening
  and reading the current directory (`getdents64`) and stat()ing
  candidate entries against the pattern, every single time, before
  giving up and falling back to the literal text. `[` is of course the
  POSIX `test`/`[ ]` conditional syntax -- one of the single most
  common tokens in any real script -- so this cost is paid on every
  `[ ... ]` use anywhere.

  Confirmed directly: `while [ $i -lt 500 ]; do i=$((i+1)); done`
  (500 iterations) makes 1002 `getdents64` + 514 `openat` (9 failing)
  syscalls; the byte-for-byte equivalent `while test $i -lt 500; do
  i=$((i+1)); done` makes zero `getdents64` calls (`test` contains no
  glob-class character, so `S_GLOB` never gets set). A broader
  20000-iteration builtin-only microbenchmark (`x="value$i"`; a `case`
  with a `value*` pattern; `test -n "$x"`; loop condition using `[`)
  runs in 1.080s under shish (sys time 0.824s -- the syscalls above
  dominate the whole run) versus bash's 0.161s (sys time 0.002s,
  effectively zero) for the identical script -- a ~6.7x gap driven
  almost entirely by this one bug, not by any general parsing/eval
  slowness. This is very likely the single largest contributor to
  shish's overall "slower than bash" gap on real-world,
  builtin/conditional-heavy scripts like autoconf-generated
  `configure` (POSIX shell test syntax uses `[ ]`, not `test`,
  essentially everywhere).

  A correct fix has to stop treating "contains a glob-class character"
  as "is a glob pattern" -- at minimum, a `[` needs a matching `]`
  later in the same word before `S_GLOB` is warranted (mirroring what
  `bre_atom_len()`/`path_fnmatch.c`'s own bracket-expression handling
  already has to do correctly elsewhere in this codebase); a real,
  more thorough fix would defer the decision to expansion time and
  check whether the accumulated pattern is even glob-syntax-plausible
  before ever calling `glob(3)`, rather than deciding once, eagerly,
  from a single raw character seen during parsing.
